"92_cocoarealize.mst — AppSpec A2: the Cocoa realizer.

 `docs/appspec.md` §5.4. This is the platform half, and it is the ONLY file in
 the framework that knows AppKit exists. It implements the realizer protocol
 declared in `world/89_apptoolwin.mst`'s header — eleven messages — and nothing
 above it may name an NSView.

 IT IS DELIBERATELY DULL, and that is the measure of whether the split worked.
 The spec model decided what exists, the layout decided where it goes, and the
 differ decided what changed; all that is left here is `create a control per
 rect, set a property per patch`. When this file starts wanting to know what a
 tool MEANS, the seam has been broken.

 THE Y-FLIP LIVES HERE, in `rectFor:` and nowhere else. Layout answers
 top-left rects because that is what the shared contract says; an unflipped
 NSView is bottom-left, so every placement is converted once on its way to a
 frame: `y_cocoa = clientHeight - y_top - h`. The bridge cannot mint a flipped
 view — `MacvmDelegate`'s roles are a fixed family and none of them is one —
 and flipping here is what the rest of this codebase already does
 (`cocoa_gui/src/canvas.rs`, `76_spriteed.mst`'s sprite row). The vendored
 layout needs no Mac edit, which is the property that matters.

 THE REALIZER OWNS EVERY NATIVE OBJECT, which is the promise `docs/appspec.md`
 makes on the framework's behalf: the window, the view tree, and — the part
 that actually bites — the ACTION DELEGATES. A `MacvmDelegate` is kept alive by
 the world-side ticket registry, but the holder blocks and the delegate handles
 are ours to drop at teardown, exactly as `70_cocoacanvas.mst` keeps its
 `ActionDelegates`. A tool author holds none of this and therefore cannot leak
 it.

 A2's CONTROL SET is the MVP the record names — text, heading, button, input,
 checkbox, divider — plus `select`, because a demo without a choice control
 proves less than it should. `slider`, `listBox` and the three pane types are
 A3's, and until then they realize as a VISIBLE placeholder rather than as
 nothing: a spec that grew a node this file has not met yet should be obviously
 wrong on screen, never invisibly absent."

Object subclass: CocoaRealize [
    <classVars: ForeignProviders>
    | toolWindow nsWin content controls delegates winDelegate panes fittedHeight |

    "── foreign views (docs/appspec.md A6) ──────────────────────────────

     THE PROVIDER REGISTRY. A `foreignView` node names a hole; a provider is
     the platform-side block that fills it, registered here under the node's
     id and answering a native view (an ObjcRef) when handed the node. The
     block lives on THIS side and never crosses a VM: the spec stays
     picklable, the tool stays portable, and only the realizer knows a real
     NSView is involved — the same division of labour a pane's pixels have.
     A provider that ANSWERS NIL declines, and the node renders as the same
     visible placeholder an unregistered one gets: a hole that says whose."
    CocoaRealize class >> provideForeign: anId <Symbol> with: aBlock <Object> [
        ForeignProviders isNil ifTrue: [ ForeignProviders := Dictionary new ].
        ForeignProviders at: anId put: aBlock.
        ^self
    ]

    CocoaRealize class >> foreignProviderFor: anId <Object> ^ <Object> [
        ForeignProviders isNil ifTrue: [ ^nil ].
        ^ForeignProviders at: anId asString asSymbol ifAbsent: [ nil ]
    ]

    CocoaRealize class >> newFor: aWindow <Object> ^ <CocoaRealize> [
        ^self new setToolWindow: aWindow
    ]

    setToolWindow: aWindow <Object> ^ <CocoaRealize> [
        toolWindow := aWindow.
        controls := OrderedCollection new.
        delegates := OrderedCollection new.
        panes := Dictionary new.
        nsWin := nil.
        fittedHeight := nil.
        ^self
    ]

    "── metrics ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

     APPROXIMATE, AND SAID SO. The layout wants {charWidth. lineHeight} for a
     proportional font, which has no single character width; measuring one
     through the bridge would cost a main-thread crossing per string and buy
     very little, because the layout uses the number only to decide how wide a
     label wants to be. They are the one place to change if text starts
     clipping, or if a window comes out the wrong size — because they decide
     both.

     THEY SET THE WINDOW'S SIZE, which is the part that is easy to miss. The
     layout derives every control's HEIGHT from lineHeight — a button is
     lineHeight + 16, an input + 10, a checkbox and a select + 12 — so a
     generous lineHeight does not merely space text out, it inflates the whole
     window. At 12pt/16 the two vendored editors came out visibly too tall.
     11pt on a 13px line is the Mac's own compact-control convention, and the
     size `76_spriteed.mst` already uses for its own labels; it brings a button
     to 29 points and a field to 23, which is what an AppKit window is supposed
     to look like.

     WIDTH ERRS HIGH, AND A SCREENSHOT SETTLED THE NUMBER. The layout
     multiplies ONE charWidth by a string's length for every text node, so the
     widest glyphs in the file set it; at 12pt a charWidth of 7 cut `data` off
     the demo's heading. Erring high is free — there is no stretch-to-fill, so
     an over-wide box is invisible while an under-wide one eats the end of a
     sentence — and 7 is generous at 11pt where it was mean at 12. The heading
     is bold at the SAME size for the same reason: the layout must never be
     asked to measure something larger than it thinks."

    fontSize ^ <Number> [ ^11.0 ]
    metrics ^ <Array> [ ^AppSpecLayout metricsCharWidth: 7 lineHeight: 13 ]

    clientWidth ^ <Integer> [ ^toolWindow width ]
    "THE HEIGHT EVERY FLIP IS TAKEN AGAINST. Before the window has been
     fitted this is what the tool asked for; afterwards it is what the content
     actually needed, so a patch that re-derives a rect lands where the
     control already is instead of jumping."
    clientHeight ^ <Integer> [
        ^fittedHeight isNil ifTrue: [ toolWindow height ] ifFalse: [ fittedHeight ]
    ]

    "── the window ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────"

    open ^ <Boolean> [
        nsWin isNil ifTrue: [ self buildWindow ].
        nsWin isNil ifTrue: [ ^false ].
        nsWin onMain makeKeyAndOrderFront: nil.
        ^true
    ]

    hide [
        nsWin isNil ifFalse: [ nsWin onMain orderOut: nil ].
        ^self
    ]

    isOpen ^ <Boolean> [
        nsWin isNil ifTrue: [ ^false ].
        ^nsWin onMain isVisible
    ]

    teardown [
        self destroyControls.
        nsWin isNil ifFalse: [
            nsWin onMain orderOut: nil.
            nsWin onMain setDelegate: nil ].
        winDelegate := nil.
        nsWin := nil.
        content := nil.
        ^self
    ]

    buildWindow [
        | winClass |
        winClass := Cocoa classNamed: 'NSWindow'.
        "styleMask 7 = titled|closable|miniaturizable. No resizable bit: the
         layout is computed for one width, and A3 is where a resize would
         re-run it."
        nsWin := ((winClass onMain alloc) onMain
            initWithContentRect: (Array with: 340.0 with: 180.0
                                        with: self clientWidth asFloat
                                        with: self clientHeight asFloat)
            styleMask: 7 backing: 2 defer: false).
        nsWin onMain setReleasedWhenClosed: false.
        nsWin onMain setTitle: toolWindow title.
        "CLOSE IS HIDE, and the framework binds it so no tool can forget —
         `windowShouldClose:` below answers false and hides instead."
        winDelegate := MacvmDelegate windowDelegateOn: self.
        nsWin onMain setDelegate: winDelegate.
        content := nsWin onMain contentView.
        content onMain setAutoresizesSubviews: true.
        ^self
    ]

    "The C6 window-delegate callback. Answering false is what keeps the tool's
     state alive across a close, which is the Mac's own choice and the
     contract's `#closeRequested` default."
    windowShouldClose: sender [
        toolWindow closeRequested.
        ^false
    ]

    "── placements -> frames ────────────────────────────────────────────"

    "THE ONE PLACE THE COORDINATE SYSTEMS MEET."
    rectFor: p <Array> ^ <Array> [
        ^Array
            with: (p at: 2) asFloat
            with: (self clientHeight - (p at: 3) - (p at: 5)) asFloat
            with: ((p at: 4) max: 1) asFloat
            with: ((p at: 5) max: 1) asFloat
    ]

    "── build ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────"

    "THE SURFACES GO WITH THE VIEWS. A rebuild replaces the pane views, so a
     plane still registered against a dead one is a leak the tool cannot see
     and cannot free — this is the realizer keeping its promise to own every
     native lifetime."
    destroyControls [
        controls do: [ :each | (each at: 1) onMain removeFromSuperview ].
        panes do: [ :h | AppSurfaces remove: h ].
        panes := Dictionary new.
        controls := OrderedCollection new.
        delegates := OrderedCollection new.
        ^self
    ]

    "The surface handle a pane node was realized into — what a tool passes to
     AppPixels/AppRender. 0 when there is no such pane, which the pixel verbs
     treat as `nothing to draw on` rather than as an error."
    paneHandleFor: nodeId <Object> ^ <Integer> [
        ^panes at: nodeId asString ifAbsent: [ 0 ]
    ]

    "A placement is {node. x. y. w. h. kind}. The KIND says which of a node's
     controls this is — its label, a card's title, or the control itself — and
     keeping them apart here is what lets `applyNodeProps:` route a patch to
     the right one later."
    createFor: p <Array> [
        | node kind view |
        content isNil ifTrue: [ ^self ].
        node := p at: 1.
        kind := p at: 6.
        kind == #label ifTrue: [
            view := self makeLabel: ((node at: #label) ifNil: [ '' ]) rect: (self rectFor: p) ].
        kind == #cardTitle ifTrue: [
            view := self makeHeading: ((node at: #title) ifNil: [ '' ]) rect: (self rectFor: p) ].
        kind == #control ifTrue: [
            view := self makeControlFor: node rect: (self rectFor: p) ].
        view isNil ifFalse: [
            controls add: (Array with: view with: node with: kind) ].
        ^self
    ]

    makeControlFor: node <AppNode> rect: rect <Array> ^ <Object> [
        | t |
        t := node type.
        t == #text ifTrue: [ ^self makeLabel: ((node at: #text) ifNil: [ '' ]) rect: rect ].
        t == #heading ifTrue: [ ^self makeHeading: ((node at: #text) ifNil: [ '' ]) rect: rect ].
        t == #button ifTrue: [ ^self makeButton: node rect: rect ].
        t == #input ifTrue: [ ^self makeInput: node rect: rect ].
        t == #checkbox ifTrue: [ ^self makeCheckbox: node rect: rect ].
        t == #divider ifTrue: [ ^self makeDivider: rect ].
        t == #select ifTrue: [ ^self makeSelect: node rect: rect ].
        t == #slider ifTrue: [ ^self makeSlider: node rect: rect ].
        t == #rgbaPane ifTrue: [ ^self makePane: node rect: rect ].
        t == #foreignView ifTrue: [ ^self makeForeign: node rect: rect ].
        "NOT YET REALIZED: say so on screen. An unimplemented node that drew
         nothing would look like a layout bug rather than a missing feature,
         and the difference matters when someone else reads it. `listBox`
         wants a real NSTableView in a scroll view; `indexedPane` and
         `textGrid` want the palette and cell-grid semantics their Windows
         shaders have. None of the three blocks A4 — the sprite editor's two
         surfaces are BOTH rgbaPane, and the sound editor's controls are
         sliders."
        ^self makeLabel: '[' , t printString , ' — not yet]' rect: rect
    ]

    "A CONTINUOUS SLIDER, which is the Mac's own idiom and simpler than the
     contract's. Win32 has to POLL a trackbar on a heartbeat because WM_HSCROLL
     is not on its door's allowlist; an NSSlider pushes its action as it moves,
     so `tick` stays empty and a drag relabels live."
    makeSlider: node <AppNode> rect: rect <Array> ^ <Object> [
        | s |
        s := ((Cocoa classNamed: 'NSSlider') onMain alloc) onMain initWithFrame: rect.
        s onMain setMinValue: ((node at: #min) ifNil: [ 0 ]) asFloat.
        s onMain setMaxValue: ((node at: #max) ifNil: [ 100 ]) asFloat.
        s onMain setDoubleValue: ((node at: #value) ifNil: [ 0 ]) asFloat.
        s onMain setContinuous: true.
        "THE VALUE CROSSES AS AN INTEGER. The contract's sliders carry integer
         positions (a Win32 trackbar cannot carry anything else), and every
         tool written against it converts with its own pure position<->value
         pair — handing over a Float here would land in that arithmetic and
         come back as a value the document never offered."
        self wire: s to: [
            toolWindow dispatch: (node at: #event)
                       value: (s onMain doubleValue) rounded ].
        content onMain addSubview: s.
        ^s
    ]

    "── panes ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

     A pane is a MacvmMouseView (an NSImageView subclass whose mouse overrides
     dispatch into the world — the role the sprite editor already uses, chosen
     because world-minted gesture recognizers never fire) plus a retained RGBA
     plane in `AppSurfaces`. The tool writes pixels through AppPixels and says
     `AppRender present:`; nothing in the tool names a view."
    makePane: node <AppNode> rect: rect <Array> ^ <Object> [
        | view w h handle |
        w := ((node at: #width) ifNil: [ 320 ]) rounded max: 1.
        h := ((node at: #height) ifNil: [ 180 ]) rounded max: 1.
        view := MacvmDelegate mouseViewOn:
            (CocoaMouseHandler on: [ :ev | self paneMouse: ev node: node view: view height: h ]).
        view onMain setFrame: rect.
        "NSImageScaleNone: the blast is already at view size, pixel for pixel."
        view onMain setImageScaling: 2.
        content onMain addSubview: view.
        handle := AppSurfaces register: w height: h present: [ :surface |
            self blast: surface to: view ].
        panes at: node idOrEmpty put: handle.
        ^view
    ]

    "THE ESCAPE HATCH REALIZED. The provider hands back a view IT built —
     alloc'd, wired, styled by hand, whatever the author wanted that the
     vocabulary cannot say — and the realizer does to it exactly what it does
     to its own controls: frame it, adopt it into the tree, and (because it
     was adopted) remove it at teardown with everything else. The author
     keeps whatever handles they like; the WINDOW's lifecycle is still owned
     here, which is the bargain the framework makes everywhere else."
    makeForeign: node <AppNode> rect: rect <Array> ^ <Object> [
        | provider view |
        provider := CocoaRealize foreignProviderFor: node idOrEmpty.
        view := provider isNil ifTrue: [ nil ] ifFalse: [
            [ provider value: node ]
                on: Error do: [ :e |
                    AppToolWindow report: 'foreign provider ' , node idOrEmpty , ': ' , e messageText.
                    nil ] ].
        view isNil ifTrue: [
            "No provider, or it declined/raised: a VISIBLE hole that says
             whose — never an invisible absence."
            ^self makeLabel: '[foreignView ' , node idOrEmpty , ' — no provider]' rect: rect ].
        view onMain setFrame: rect.
        content onMain addSubview: view.
        ^view
    ]

    "BGRA IN THE PLANE, RGBA ON THE WIRE. The plane's byte order is Windows'
     so that a tool's `bgraR:g:b:` arithmetic is identical on both platforms
     (see world/93_apppixels.mst); the canvas host verb wants RGBA, so the
     shuffle happens HERE, once per present, in the one place that is allowed
     to know about either."
    blast: surface <Object> to: view <Object> [
        | plane out n |
        plane := surface plane.
        plane isNil ifTrue: [ ^self ].
        n := plane size.
        out := ByteArray new: n.
        0 to: n - 4 by: 4 do: [ :i |
            out at: i + 1 put: (plane at: i + 3).   "R <- byte 3"
            out at: i + 2 put: (plane at: i + 2).   "G"
            out at: i + 3 put: (plane at: i + 1).   "B <- byte 1"
            out at: i + 4 put: (plane at: i + 4) ].
        CocoaBrowser hostService onMain
            showCanvasPixelsOn: view base64: (Base64 encode: out)
            width: surface width printString
            height: surface height printString.
        ^self
    ]

    "A PANE'S POINT ARRIVES IN THE PANE'S OWN PIXELS, TOP-LEFT — the same
     space `invalidatePane:rect:` speaks, which is what lets a handler say
     `I touched this cell` in the coordinates it was just handed. AppKit
     gives it bottom-left, so the flip happens here."
    paneMouse: ev <Object> node: node <AppNode> view: aView height: h <Integer> [
        | pt x y |
        pt := self viewPoint: ev in: aView.
        x := (pt at: 1) asInteger.
        y := h - 1 - (pt at: 2) asInteger.
        ^toolWindow dispatch: (node at: #event) value: (Array with: x with: y)
    ]

    viewPoint: ev <Object> in: aView <Object> ^ <Array> [
        | wp |
        wp := ev send: 'locationInWindow' args: (Array new: 0) ret: #point.
        ^aView send: 'convertPoint:fromView:'
               args: (Array with: (wp at: 1) with: (wp at: 2) with: nil)
               ret: #point
    ]

    makeLabel: aString <String> rect: rect <Array> ^ <Object> [
        | l |
        l := ((Cocoa classNamed: 'NSTextField') onMain alloc) onMain initWithFrame: rect.
        l onMain setEditable: false.
        l onMain setBordered: false.
        l onMain setDrawsBackground: false.
        l onMain setSelectable: false.
        l onMain setFont: ((Cocoa classNamed: 'NSFont') onMain systemFontOfSize: self fontSize).
        l onMain setStringValue: aString asString.
        content onMain addSubview: l.
        ^l
    ]

    "BOLD AT THE SAME SIZE, not larger. The layout measures every text node
     with one charWidth, so a heading rendered a point bigger is a heading the
     layout under-measured — weight alone reads as a heading and costs no extra
     width the arithmetic does not know about."
    makeHeading: aString <String> rect: rect <Array> ^ <Object> [
        | l |
        l := self makeLabel: aString rect: rect.
        l onMain setFont:
            ((Cocoa classNamed: 'NSFont') onMain boldSystemFontOfSize: self fontSize).
        ^l
    ]

    "THE FRAME IS SET AFTER THE BOX TYPE, and the first screenshot is why: an
     NSBox configured after construction re-derives its geometry from its
     title and border, so a separator built at its final frame came out a dot
     in the corner instead of a rule across the card. Type first, then frame."
    makeDivider: rect <Array> ^ <Object> [
        | box |
        box := ((Cocoa classNamed: 'NSBox') onMain alloc) onMain initWithFrame: rect.
        "NSBoxSeparator = 2."
        box onMain setBoxType: 2.
        box onMain setTitlePosition: 0.   "NSNoTitle"
        box onMain setFrame:
            (Array with: (rect at: 1) with: (rect at: 2) + ((rect at: 4) / 2.0)
                   with: (rect at: 3) with: 1.0).
        content onMain addSubview: box.
        ^box
    ]

    makeButton: node <AppNode> rect: rect <Array> ^ <Object> [
        | btn |
        btn := ((Cocoa classNamed: 'NSButton') onMain alloc) onMain initWithFrame: rect.
        btn onMain setTitle: ((node at: #text) ifNil: [ '' ]) asString.
        btn onMain setBezelStyle: 1.
        btn onMain setFont: ((Cocoa classNamed: 'NSFont') onMain systemFontOfSize: self fontSize).
        self wire: btn to: [ toolWindow dispatch: (node at: #event) value: nil ].
        content onMain addSubview: btn.
        ^btn
    ]

    makeInput: node <AppNode> rect: rect <Array> ^ <Object> [
        | f |
        f := ((Cocoa classNamed: 'NSTextField') onMain alloc) onMain initWithFrame: rect.
        f onMain setFont: ((Cocoa classNamed: 'NSFont') onMain systemFontOfSize: self fontSize).
        f onMain setStringValue: ((node at: #value) ifNil: [ '' ]) asString.
        "An NSTextField's action fires on Enter and on focus loss, which is the
         Mac's own idiom; the contract's `every keystroke` is Win32's EN_CHANGE
         and is not worth a text delegate per field here."
        self wire: f to: [ toolWindow dispatch: (node at: #event) value: (f onMain stringValue) ].
        content onMain addSubview: f.
        ^f
    ]

    makeCheckbox: node <AppNode> rect: rect <Array> ^ <Object> [
        | b |
        b := ((Cocoa classNamed: 'NSButton') onMain alloc) onMain initWithFrame: rect.
        "NSButtonTypeSwitch = 3. A checkbox draws its OWN title beside the box,
         which is why the layout exempts it from the label rule — a STATIC
         above it would render the word twice."
        b onMain setButtonType: 3.
        b onMain setTitle: ((node at: #label) ifNil: [ '' ]) asString.
        b onMain setFont: ((Cocoa classNamed: 'NSFont') onMain systemFontOfSize: self fontSize).
        b onMain setState: ((node at: #checked) == true ifTrue: [ 1 ] ifFalse: [ 0 ]).
        self wire: b to: [
            toolWindow dispatch: (node at: #event)
                       value: ((b onMain state) = 1) ].
        content onMain addSubview: b.
        ^b
    ]

    makeSelect: node <AppNode> rect: rect <Array> ^ <Object> [
        | popup |
        popup := ((Cocoa classNamed: 'NSPopUpButton') onMain alloc) onMain
            initWithFrame: rect pullsDown: false.
        popup onMain setFont: ((Cocoa classNamed: 'NSFont') onMain systemFontOfSize: self fontSize).
        self fill: popup from: node.
        "A SELECTION CROSSES AS THE OPTION'S VALUE, never its text and never its
         index — the value is what the spec said and what the handler will
         compare against. `AppSpec optionValue:` is the one place that knows."
        self wire: popup to: [
            toolWindow dispatch: (node at: #event)
                       value: (self valueChosenIn: popup from: node) ].
        content onMain addSubview: popup.
        ^popup
    ]

    fill: popup <Object> from: node <AppNode> [
        | opts idx |
        popup onMain removeAllItems.
        opts := (node at: #options) ifNil: [ #() ].
        opts do: [ :opt | popup onMain addItemWithTitle: (AppSpec optionText: opt) asString ].
        idx := AppSpec indexOfValue: (node at: #value) in: opts.
        idx < 0 ifTrue: [ ^self ].
        popup onMain selectItemAtIndex: idx.
        ^self
    ]

    valueChosenIn: popup <Object> from: node <AppNode> ^ <Object> [
        | opts idx |
        opts := (node at: #options) ifNil: [ #() ].
        idx := popup onMain indexOfSelectedItem.
        (idx < 0 or: [ idx >= opts size ]) ifTrue: [ ^nil ].
        ^AppSpec optionValue: (opts at: idx + 1)
    ]

    "Every control gets its own zero-argument holder block, which is the
     bridge's shape (`CocoaToolbarAction>>macvmAction:` answers `Block value`)
     and is exactly what a realizer wants: the block closes over the node, so
     there is no tag to look up and no table to keep in step."
    wire: aControl <Object> to: aBlock <Object> [
        | holder delegate |
        holder := CocoaToolbarAction on: aBlock.
        delegate := MacvmDelegate actionTargetOn: holder.
        aControl onMain setTarget: delegate.
        aControl onMain setAction: 'macvmAction:'.
        delegates add: delegate.
        ^self
    ]

    "── patch ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────"

    applyWindowProps: changed <OrderedCollection> [
        nsWin isNil ifTrue: [ ^self ].
        changed do: [ :a |
            a key == #title ifTrue: [ nsWin onMain setTitle: a value asString ] ].
        ^self
    ]

    "EACH PROP GOES TO ITS OWN CONTROL. WINARMVM's realizer picked ONE control
     for a patch's whole changed-list, and the bug that produced is worth
     carrying the warning for: a slider's patch changes both its label and its
     value, the label control won, the value went to a STATIC as a harmless
     no-op, and the thumb stayed put — after which the poll read the stale
     thumb and pushed the OLD value back into the document. The screen quietly
     reverting the model, with nothing raising anywhere."
    applyNodeProps: changed <OrderedCollection> to: nodeId <String> [
        changed do: [ :a | self applyProp: a to: nodeId ].
        ^self
    ]

    applyProp: a <Object> to: nodeId <String> [
        | kind pair |
        kind := a key == #title
            ifTrue: [ #cardTitle ]
            ifFalse: [ a key == #label ifTrue: [ #label ] ifFalse: [ #control ] ].
        pair := self controlFor: nodeId kind: kind.
        "A label that a node does not actually have (a checkbox's, which the
         layout deliberately never places) simply has nowhere to go."
        pair isNil ifTrue: [ ^self ].
        "THE STORED NODE IS BROUGHT UP TO DATE FIRST, and a screenshot is why.
         `controls` holds the node as it was when the control was CREATED, and
         most props are applied straight from the patch — but a choice control
         is refilled FROM ITS NODE, because a selection is an index into that
         node's options. Reading the stale node meant the demo's colour popup
         still said `Red` after the model had moved to Blue: the patch arrived,
         the control was refilled, and it was refilled with yesterday's answer.
         Keeping the stored node in step is the realizer's half of the host-echo
         rule — what it holds must match what is on screen."
        (pair at: 2) at: a key put: a value.
        self set: a on: (pair at: 1) node: (pair at: 2) kind: kind.
        ^self
    ]

    set: a <Object> on: view <Object> node: node <AppNode> kind: aKind <Symbol> [
        | t |
        "A label and a card title are STATICs whatever their node is."
        aKind == #control ifFalse: [ ^view onMain setStringValue: a value asString ].
        t := node type.
        a key == #text ifTrue: [
            t == #button
                ifTrue: [ ^view onMain setTitle: a value asString ]
                ifFalse: [
                    view onMain setStringValue: a value asString.
                    ^self widen: view toFit: a value asString ] ].
        a key == #value ifTrue: [
            t == #input ifTrue: [ ^view onMain setStringValue: a value asString ].
            t == #select ifTrue: [ ^self fill: view from: node ].
            ^self ].
        a key == #checked ifTrue: [
            ^view onMain setState: (a value == true ifTrue: [ 1 ] ifFalse: [ 0 ]) ].
        a key == #options ifTrue: [
            t == #select ifTrue: [ ^self fill: view from: node ].
            ^self ].
        ^self
    ]

    "A PATCH MAY CARRY LONGER TEXT THAN THE CONTROL REALIZED AT — the layout
     measured the spec of the moment, and a later state has no duty to stay
     short (the Scale of Everything''s heading patched from ''Your body'' to
     ''Surface of the sun'' and lost its sun). The differ cannot say ''and
     now re-measure''; only this side knows glyph widths. So a text patch
     keeps the frame when the new text fits and GROWS the frame when it does
     not — never shrinks (a static has no stretch-to-fill, so erring wide is
     free, and shrink-grow cycles would read as a tic). Height stays: growth
     is horizontal, the direction a one-line static clips in."
    widen: view <Object> toFit: aString <String> [
        | cell size w frame |
        cell := view onMain cell.
        cell isNil ifTrue: [ ^self ].
        size := cell onMain cellSize.
        (size isKindOf: Array) ifFalse: [ ^self ].
        w := (size at: 1) + 4.
        frame := view onMain frame.
        (frame at: 3) >= w ifTrue: [ ^self ].
        view onMain setFrameSize: (Array with: w with: (frame at: 4)).
        ^self
    ]

    controlFor: nodeId <String> kind: aKind <Symbol> ^ <Object> [
        controls do: [ :each |
            (((each at: 2) idOrEmpty = nodeId) and: [ (each at: 3) == aKind ])
                ifTrue: [ ^each ] ].
        ^nil
    ]

    "── fit the window to what was actually laid out ────────────────────

     A TOOL'S DECLARED SIZE IS A GUESS MADE ON ANOTHER PLATFORM. Both vendored
     editors ask for 720 points tall, computed against Win32's font; ours is
     taller per line, so the same spec needs more room and the windows came out
     cramped — the sprite editor's rightmost button clipped off the edge, both
     of them taller than their content wanted to be. Nothing about that is the
     tools' fault and nothing about it can be fixed in a spec, because the spec
     does not know the font.

     So the realizer measures. Layout has just answered every rect, so the
     content's true extent is known exactly: take the furthest edge, add the
     root padding back, and size the window to THAT. A tool that declared too
     much shrinks, one that declared too little grows, and neither has to know
     which platform it is on. The declared width still drives LAYOUT — it is
     what the columns were composed against — so only the window follows.

     Clamped to something a screen can hold, because a tool is free to declare
     a spec taller than the display and a window that cannot be reached is
     worse than one that scrolls off."
    layoutFinished: places <Object> [
        | maxX maxY w h |
        (nsWin isNil or: [ places isNil ]) ifTrue: [ ^self ].
        maxX := 0.
        maxY := 0.
        places do: [ :p |
            maxX := maxX max: (p at: 2) + (p at: 4).
            maxY := maxY max: (p at: 3) + (p at: 5) ].
        maxX = 0 ifTrue: [ ^self ].
        w := (maxX + AppSpecLayout rootPadding) min: 1600.
        h := (maxY + AppSpecLayout rootPadding) min: 1000.
        w := w max: 200.
        h := h max: 120.
        "THE HEIGHT MUST BE SET BEFORE THE CONTROLS ARE PLACED, and they
         already are — so the frames move with it. `rectFor:` flipped every y
         against the OLD client height, so changing it now would put every
         control at the wrong height unless they are re-derived. Re-place them
         against the new height rather than laying out again: same rects, one
         subtraction different."
        self resizeContentTo: w height: h places: places.
        ^self
    ]

    resizeContentTo: w <Integer> height: h <Integer> places: places <Object> [
        | frame |
        frame := nsWin onMain frame.
        "setContentSize: keeps the top-left where it is on screen, which is
         what a user expects of a window that fits itself."
        nsWin onMain setContentSize: (Array with: w asFloat with: h asFloat).
        nsWin onMain setFrameTopLeftPoint:
            (Array with: (frame at: 1) with: (frame at: 2) + (frame at: 4)).
        fittedHeight := h.
        places do: [ :p | self repositionFor: p ].
        ^self
    ]

    "One control, moved to where it belongs under the window's real height."
    repositionFor: p <Array> [
        | pair |
        pair := self controlFor: (p at: 1) idOrEmpty kind: (p at: 6).
        pair isNil ifTrue: [ ^self ].
        (pair at: 1) onMain setFrame: (self rectFor: p).
        ^self
    ]

    "Nothing to poll: a Cocoa control pushes its action when it changes, where
     a Win32 trackbar has to be read on a heartbeat. A3's panes may want this."
    tick [ ^self ]

    "── for tests and for driving it ────────────────────────────────────"

    controls ^ <OrderedCollection> [ ^controls ]
    nsWindow ^ <Object> [ ^nsWin ]

    placedIds ^ <OrderedCollection> [
        | out |
        out := OrderedCollection new.
        controls do: [ :each |
            ((each at: 3) == #control) ifTrue: [ out add: (each at: 2) idOrEmpty ] ].
        ^out
    ]

    "What a control actually SHOWS, read back from AppKit — the only honest way
     to assert that a patch reached the screen rather than merely the model."
    shownTextFor: nodeId <String> ^ <Object> [
        | pair view |
        pair := self controlFor: nodeId kind: #control.
        pair isNil ifTrue: [ ^nil ].
        view := pair at: 1.
        ((pair at: 2) type == #button)
            ifTrue: [ ^view onMain title ]
            ifFalse: [ ^view onMain stringValue ]
    ]
]

"NO TRAILING DOIT INSTALLS THIS, deliberately. `AppToolWindow class >>
 realizerClass` resolves this class BY NAME the first time a window needs it,
 because the GUI boots its VMs from the image and image boot replays only a
 two-entry WORLD_DOITS list — a `realizerClass: CocoaRealize.` line here would
 load, look right, and never run. Merely being in cocoaui.list is therefore
 the whole installation: this file exists in the UI worker's world, so the UI
 worker draws, and every VM without it keeps the null realizer."
